{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310807,"nonce":"y7XucF5O8f1sK43K+8DszXQj0C0=","title":"Ported llama.cpp's ggml_vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K NEON pattern at commit c5ab136. +15.4% on real 80-tok decode (median 28.06 vs 24.33 tok/s ...","message":"Standalone microbench (`/tmp/q4km_paired_bench.rs`, L1-resident, no FFI):\n\n|  | ns/super-block | Ratio |\n|---|---|---|\n| Our `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock` | 16.34 | 1.00x |\n| Our 2-block paired (acb80e5) | 15.21 | 0.93x |\n| Our 4-block paired | 15.90 | 0.97x |\n| **llama.cpp pattern (ported)** | **7.69** | **0.47x (2.12x faster)** |\n\nReal 80-tok decode A/B, canonical Paris-history prompt:\n\n|  | Median tok/s | Range | Sign |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NEW (llama.cpp port) | **28.06** | 26.79..29.12 | 5-of-5 |\n| LEGACY (2-block paired) | 24.33 | 23.59..25.19 | — |\n| Delta | **+15.4%** | | |\n\nMicrobench 2.12x kernel speedup → only +15.4% on real decode. The\nremaining ~30% of decode wall (attention, qkv, sample, dispatch, FFI,\nworker pool fork-join) doesn't benefit from the kernel change.\n\n\n1. **Scale decode ONCE per super-block** — bit-twiddle utmp[4] into a\n   u8[8] `scales` array up front; index `scales[2*j]` inside the\n   inner loop instead of calling `q4_k_get_scale_min` 4x.\n2. **bsums × mins in i16→i32 path, computed ONCE** — vpaddq_s16 +\n   2x vmull_s16 + vaddvq_s32 + scalar `dmin * sum as f32`.\n   Replaces 4x f32 multiplications per p-iter with a single i32\n   reduction once.\n3. **Per-sub-block accumulation in i32** — `sumi1 += vaddvq(p1) *\n   scales[j]` stays integer; f32 conversion deferred to a SINGLE\n   `sumf += d * (sumi1 + sumi2) as f32` at the end.\n4. **Serial SDOT chain** (not 4-way split-acc unroll) — M1's OoO\n   scheduler extracts the ILP from the serial chain anyway. The\n   a241cc9 unroll was paying register pressure for parallelism the\n   hardware already provides; without f32-conversion churn the\n   serial chain wins.\n\n\nf32 reduction order changes by 1-2 ULP. Paris MATCH preserved both\nwith new kernel (default) and `RAYZOR_LEGACY_KERNEL=1`. Could differ\non a different prompt; memory `bugs_llama_chat_match_overstated` notes\nMATCH only held on canonical prompt anyway.\n\n\n- llama.cpp CPU: 87 tok/s (per perf_llama_cpp_gap_measured)\n- rayzor at c5ab136: 28 tok/s\n- Remaining gap: **3.1x**\n\nWhat it lives in (per A's earlier measurement of per-matmul-call):\n- Setup (alloc, qmatmul_prep, parallel_rows construction): 7.5 µs/call\n- Worker pool fork-join sync overhead: estimated 50-80 µs/call (NOT\n  directly measured; needs per-worker active_us histogram to confirm)\n- FFI bridge cost: not yet measured at the matmul boundary\n- Inner SDOT work per worker: 187 µs/call mean\n\nThe kernel work is done. The next lever is structural: persistent\nworker pool with single fork-join per token (compute graph), or\ncollapsing all per-layer matmuls into a single FFI call that\ninternally dispatches. Both are multi-hour investigations.\n\n\nThe `tools/llama-diff/bench.sh` empirical A/B (perf_llama_cpp_gap_measured)\n+ standalone kernel microbench + bench-the-fix-on-real-decode is the\ndiscipline that worked. The 6 prior wash-outs in the session all skipped\nthe \"microbench before mirroring\" step that this win started with.\n\nCost: ~30 min microbench design + ~10 min builds + ~10 min A/B runs.\nWin: +15.4% on real decode (largest of the session).\n\n\n- runtime/src/quant.rs: new `dot_q4_k_q8_kblock_llamacpp` + wiring in\n  `qmatmul_chunk_impl_sdot_q4km` with RAYZOR_LEGACY_KERNEL=1 fallback\n- /tmp/q4km_paired_bench.rs: extended microbench (kept for future\n  kernel investigations)\n\n\nRevert via `git reset --hard llamacpp-bench-baseline` (tag at 0d80ffd)\nif any regression surfaces. Or set `RAYZOR_LEGACY_KERNEL=1` to\nruntime-fall back to the 2-block paired path while keeping all other\nsession commits.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_q4km_llamacpp_kernel_port` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}